\description{
Uses a greedy, single-pass search based on multivariate
\code{\link[mvMORPH]{mvgls}()} fits. One-shift candidates are ranked once and
then tested in that fixed order. The selected configuration is not guaranteed
to be globally optimal. Optional per-shift IC weights compare the final model
with refits that each omit one accepted shift.

The function fits the supplied response variables directly; it does not perform
PCA. Models assume multi-rate Brownian motion with proportional VCV scaling
across regimes.
}
\details{
\strong{Input requirements.}
\itemize{
\item \emph{Tree:} \code{baseline_tree} should be a rooted \code{phylo} tree
with branch lengths expressed on a common scale. An ultrametric tree is not required.
The starting tree does not need to already be painted; \code{searchOptimalConfiguration()}
paints a single baseline regime internally before building shifted candidates.
\item \emph{Trait data alignment:} \code{rownames(trait_data)} must match
\code{baseline_tree$tip.label} in both names and order; any tips without data should be
pruned beforehand.
\item \emph{Data type:} \code{trait_data} is typically a numeric matrix of continuous traits;
numeric response-only \code{data.frame}s are also supported for intercept-only
searches, and named mixed-type \code{data.frame}s are supported for richer
formulas. High-dimensional settings (p \eqn{\ge} n) are supported via
penalized-likelihood \code{mvgls()} fits.
}

\strong{Search outline.}
Fit the single-regime baseline and each eligible one-shift candidate. Rank
candidates once by their IC improvement over the baseline. Visit that fixed
list once, fitting each proposal on the current accepted tree. Accept it when
its improvement over the current accepted model meets
\code{shift_acceptance_threshold}. Rejected candidates are not revisited after
later acceptances. Optional IC weights compare the final model with a refit
omitting each accepted shift, using \code{\link[mvMORPH]{aicw}}.

If no eligible non-root candidates remain, or no proposed shifts are accepted,
the returned final model is the single-regime baseline and \code{optimal_ic}
equals \code{baseline_ic}.

\strong{Search-setting diagnostics.}
\code{min_descendant_tips} filters candidate nodes using their original number
of descendant tips. It is applied only when the initial candidate pool is built;
it is not reapplied to the resulting regimes after nested shifts are accepted.
Consequently, a resulting regime can contain fewer tips than the threshold.
An eligible small clade is evaluated like any other candidate and must pass
\code{shift_acceptance_threshold}.
Under Brownian motion, high trait disparity concentrated on short branches implies a high
evolutionary-rate estimate, but a runtime rule cannot determine whether that pattern is
biological or unstable. The permissive-settings advisory is therefore a guardrail rather
than a power calculation. Examine per-shift IC weights by enabling \code{uncertaintyweights}
or \code{uncertaintyweights_par}, repeat searches across plausible settings, and use
dataset-specific simulations when conclusions depend on individual shifts.

\strong{Parallelization.} \code{num_cores} caps simultaneous model fits: candidate scoring
and parallel IC-weight re-estimation may use all requested workers, while the greedy
search and serial IC weights always run one fit at a time. With progress enabled, fits
run in Future workers so the main process can poll and redraw the spinner without
advancing completion. Where forking is supported on Unix outside \code{RStudio},
\code{multicore} is used; otherwise \code{multisession} is used. Numerical-thread limits
depend on the number of fitting workers, not on \code{progress}. With multiple workers,
thread-limit environment variables and \pkg{RhpcBLASctl} runtime controls request one
numerical thread in each worker. Single-worker stages preserve the caller's thread-limit
environment, allowing within-fit multithreading when supported by the numerical library.
For consistent behavior across fresh R sessions, configure numerical-thread environment
variables before starting R; runtime-only changes in the main process are not automatically
copied to \code{multisession} workers. Stage workers are shut down and the previous
thread-limit environment and Future plan are restored afterward. Workers
signal conditions through \pkg{progressr}; only the main R process renders progress,
verbose messages, and plots.

\strong{Plotting.} If \code{plot = TRUE}, trees are rendered with
\code{\link[phytools]{plotSimmap}()}; shift IDs are labeled with \code{\link[ape]{nodelabels}()}.

\strong{Regime VCVs.} For a multi-regime model, the returned \code{$VCVs}
contain regime-specific covariance estimates extracted via \code{extractRegimeVCVs}.
When no shifts are accepted, the final model is single-regime \code{"BM"}:
its trees retain baseline label \code{"0"}, and \code{VCVs[["0"]]} contains
the global covariance matrix from \code{model_no_uncertainty$sigma$Pinv},
including regularization when used. This entry does not represent an inferred
shift or an additional fit; the model's \code{param} field remains \code{NA}.

\strong{Fitting methods.} Choose an \code{mvgls} method appropriate for the
formula and data. \code{"H&L"} with the \code{"RidgeArch"} penalty is restricted
to intercept-only models. For models with predictors, \code{"LL"} is one option
when an unregularized covariance can be estimated; \code{"PL-LOOCV"} is a
penalized alternative. When \code{IC = "BIC"} and \code{method} is omitted,
bifrost supplies \code{method = "LL"}. For \code{IC = "GIC"}, an omitted
method is left to the installed \pkg{mvMORPH} default. See
\code{\link[mvMORPH]{mvgls}} for method and penalty choices.
}
\section{Convergence and robustness}{

The search is greedy and visits candidates in an initial ranked order. The
selected configuration is not guaranteed to minimize the information
criterion over all possible shift configurations.

Numerical fits can depend on starting values and optimizer stopping criteria
with either \code{method = "H&L"} or \code{method = "LL"}. For conclusions
that depend on individual shifts, repeat the search with the same data, tree,
formula, and search settings using several recorded seeds when initialization
is stochastic. Comparisons of supported \code{start_strategy} values can
provide a separate initialization-sensitivity check. Record the software
versions and parallel settings, and compare the accepted shifts,
information-criterion values, and fitted rates.

Inspect optimizer codes and messages for the final model and saved forward
proposals when available. A fit with a nonzero optimizer convergence code
can still contribute an IC to candidate ranking or a forward acceptance
decision. A nonzero code indicates that the optimizer reported a warning or
failure; code 0 does not establish a global optimum. Inspect optimizer codes
and messages rather than relying on \code{warnings} alone.
Agreement across repeated runs is useful evidence of stability, but changing
the seed may not change a deterministic initialization.

Compact example objects may omit the forward-proposal fits.

Separately, vary \code{shift_acceptance_threshold},
\code{min_descendant_tips}, or the information criterion to assess sensitivity
to search settings. Per-shift IC weights are conditional on the selected
fitted model; they do not assess convergence or alternative search paths.
}

\examples{
library(ape)
library(phytools)
library(mvMORPH)
set.seed(1)

# Simulate a tree
tr <- pbtree(n = 50, scale = 1)

# Define baseline regime "0" and high-rate regime "1" on a subset of tips
states <- setNames(rep("0", Ntip(tr)), tr$tip.label)
high_clade_tips <- tr$tip.label[1:20]
states[high_clade_tips] <- "1"

# Make a SIMMAP tree for the BMM simulation
simmap <- phytools::make.simmap(tr, states, model = "ER", nsim = 1)

# Simulate traits under a BMM model with ~10x higher rate in regime "1"
sigma <- list(
  "0" = diag(0.1, 2),
  "1" = diag(1.0, 2)
)
theta <- c(0, 0)

sim <- mvMORPH::mvSIM(
  tree  = simmap,
  nsim  = 1,
  model = "BMM",
  param = list(
    ntraits = 2,
    sigma   = sigma,
    theta   = theta
  )
)

# mvSIM returns either a matrix or a list of matrices depending on mvMORPH version
X <- if (is.list(sim)) sim[[1]] else sim
rownames(X) <- simmap$tip.label

# Run the search on the unpainted single-regime tree
res <- searchOptimalConfiguration(
  baseline_tree              = as.phylo(simmap),
  trait_data                 = X,
  formula                    = "trait_data ~ 1",
  min_descendant_tips        = 10,
  num_cores                  = 1,   # keep it simple / CRAN-safe
  shift_acceptance_threshold = 20,  # conservative GIC threshold
  IC                         = "GIC",
  plot                       = FALSE,
  store_model_fit_history    = TRUE,
  verbose                    = FALSE,
  progress                   = FALSE
)

res$shift_nodes_no_uncertainty
res$baseline_ic - res$optimal_ic  # total IC improvement
res$model_no_uncertainty$opt[c("convergence", "message")]
head(icTrajectory(res))           # forward proposals and decisions

\donttest{
# Intercept-only empirical-style search:
# H&L + measurement error
res_hl <- searchOptimalConfiguration(
  baseline_tree              = as.phylo(simmap),
  trait_data                 = X,
  formula                    = "trait_data ~ 1",
  min_descendant_tips        = 10,
  num_cores                  = 1,
  shift_acceptance_threshold = 20,
  IC                         = "GIC",
  plot                       = FALSE,
  method                     = "H&L",
  error                      = TRUE,
  store_model_fit_history    = FALSE,
  verbose                    = FALSE,
  progress                   = FALSE
)

# Formula-based search with a predictor:
# LL is one option for a model with predictors
dat <- data.frame(
  trait1    = X[, 1],
  trait2    = X[, 2],
  predictor = rnorm(nrow(X))
)
rownames(dat) <- simmap$tip.label

res_ll <- searchOptimalConfiguration(
  baseline_tree              = as.phylo(simmap),
  trait_data                 = dat,
  formula                    = "trait_data[, 1:2] ~ trait_data[, 3]",
  min_descendant_tips        = 10,
  num_cores                  = 1,
  shift_acceptance_threshold = 20,
  IC                         = "GIC",
  plot                       = FALSE,
  method                     = "LL",
  error                      = TRUE,
  store_model_fit_history    = FALSE,
  verbose                    = FALSE,
  progress                   = FALSE
)
}
}
\references{
Berv, J. S. et al. (2026). Rates of passerine body plan evolution in time
and space. \emph{Nature Ecology & Evolution}.
doi:10.1038/s41559-026-03110-5.
}
